    name: "APUSH DBQ on the Erie Canal, numbered documents, teacher heading",
    expect: "dbq",
    text: `Priya Raman
Mr. Okonjo
APUSH Period 4
14 November 2026

DBQ: To what extent did the Erie Canal transform the economy of the United States between 1825 and 1850?

When the Erie Canal opened in 1825, many people still called it "Clinton's Ditch," a joke about Governor DeWitt Clinton spending state money on a trench across the wilderness. Within twenty-five years the joke had stopped being funny to anyone who had bet against it. The canal transformed the economy of the northern United States by collapsing the cost of shipping, by turning New York City into the country's commercial capital, and by pulling the farms of the Old Northwest into eastern markets, although its benefits were uneven and fell hardest on the people and regions it bypassed.

The most immediate change was the cost of moving goods. Document 1, a table of freight rates, shows that shipping a ton of flour from Buffalo to New York fell from roughly one hundred dollars by wagon to under ten dollars by canal boat within a decade, while the trip shrank from about three weeks to just over one. In Document 2, a merchant writes to his brother that grain he once fed to his pigs can now be sold in Albany at a profit. This shows that the canal did not just make old trade cheaper; it created trade that had not existed at all. Because the writer is a merchant who profited directly, he may exaggerate, but the freight rates in Document 1 support his basic claim.

The canal also changed which city would dominate the nation's commerce. Document 4, a chart of the value of goods passing through major ports, shows New York pulling far ahead of Philadelphia, Baltimore and Boston after 1825. Document 5, an engraving of the harbor in 1840, shows a forest of masts and warehouses along South Street. Philadelphia tried to compete with its own Main Line canal over the Allegheny Mountains, but as Document 6 notes, that route needed a portage railroad to haul boats over the ridges, and it never matched the Erie's volume. Geography gave New York a water-level route west, and the canal turned that advantage into dominance.

Farther west, the canal tied Ohio, Indiana, Michigan and Illinois to the Atlantic economy. Document 3, a map of towns founded along the canal and the Great Lakes, shows settlements like Rochester, Syracuse and Cleveland growing rapidly. Farmers who had once floated their crops south to New Orleans began shipping east instead. This shift had political consequences as well as economic ones: by 1850 the Northwest was linked to the Northeast by trade, which helps explain why those regions stood together when the sectional crisis came.

However, the transformation was not equally good for everyone. Document 7, a petition from New England farmers, complains that cheap western wheat arriving by canal has made their rocky hillside farms unprofitable, and many of their sons left for mill towns or for the West. Document 8, a description of the laborers who dug the canal, describes men working in waist-deep water for low wages and dying of fever in the Montezuma marshes. The canal's prosperity was built on dangerous, poorly paid work, and the profits mostly went to merchants and landowners rather than the people who dug it. The Haudenosaunee nations whose land the route crossed are almost absent from the documents, which is itself telling about whose story they record.

In conclusion, the Erie Canal transformed the American economy to a great extent. It lowered shipping costs dramatically, made New York the center of American trade, and bound the West to the East. Yet as the petition and the laborers' accounts show, the same changes that enriched New York City and western farmers impoverished older farming regions and depended on exploited workers. The ditch that people had laughed at ended up redrawing the economic map of the country.`,
