    text: `1918 Flu DBQ
Prompt: Evaluate how effectively American cities responded to the influenza pandemic of 1918.
In the fall of 1918 a new strain of influenza spread across the United States while the country was still fighting World War I. Cities had to decide quickly whether to close schools, ban public gatherings and require masks. Some cities acted fast and some waited, and the documents show that the cities that acted early and kept their rules in place longer generally did better, although public resistance and the war effort made every response harder.
Philadelphia is the clearest example of a city that responded too slowly. According to Doc A, city officials allowed a Liberty Loan parade to go ahead on September 28 even though sailors in the city were already sick. Doc B, a graph of weekly deaths, shows Philadelphia's death rate spiking within about ten days of the parade and reaching one of the highest peaks in the country. The officials probably let the parade happen because selling war bonds was seen as patriotic, which shows how the war got in the way of public health.
St. Louis responded much faster. Doc C is an order from the St. Louis health commissioner closing schools, theaters, churches and saloons in early October, only days after the first civilian cases. On the same graph in Doc B, the St. Louis line rises but stays far lower than Philadelphia's. This suggests that closing public places early really did slow the spread. However, Doc B only shows two cities, so it is possible that other factors like weather or population density mattered too.
Not everyone accepted the rules. Doc D is a newspaper cartoon from San Francisco showing a man arguing with a policeman about wearing a gauze mask. San Francisco required masks in public, and according to Doc E some residents formed an "Anti-Mask League" and others were fined or jailed. When the city lifted its mask order too early, cases came back in a second wave. This shows that a good policy only works if people follow it and if the city does not give up too soon.
Finally, Doc F, a letter from a nurse in Chicago, describes hospitals so full that patients lay in hallways and nurses were getting sick themselves. Even cities with good policies did not have enough doctors and nurses, because so many were serving overseas. No city could fully protect its people when the medical system was this overwhelmed.
Overall, American cities responded with mixed success. Cities like St. Louis that closed public places early and kept them closed had fewer deaths, while Philadelphia's parade shows what happened when a city waited. But public pushback, as in San Francisco, and a shortage of medical workers because of the war limited what any city could do.`,
