    name: "Rhymed poem about a late-October garden, fully punctuated",
    expect: "poem",
    text: `Inventory of a Late October Garden

The tomatoes split their skins in last night's rain
and lean like tired men against the stakes;
the basil, blackened, will not rise again,
whatever promises the morning makes.

The squash vines, which in July had meant to climb
the fence and claim the neighbor's yard as well,
lie flat and leathered, done with all their time,
each great leaf curling like an empty shell.

But look: the kale stands up, blue-green and proud,
as if the frost were something it had planned;
the marigolds still burn against the cloud;
a single pepper reddens in my hand.

So let me be the kind of thing that stays,
that toughens in the cold and sweetens, too,
and keeps a little color through the grays
until the ground is ready for the new.`,
