    name: "Biology class notes on cellular respiration in nested bullets",
    expect: "notes",
    text: `Bio notes 10/7 — Cellular Respiration
● Big idea: cells break down glucose to make ATP (usable energy)
○ C6H12O6 + 6O2 → 6CO2 + 6H2O + ATP
○ basically the opposite of photosynthesis
● 3 stages
○ Glycolysis
■ in the cytoplasm
■ glucose → 2 pyruvate
■ makes 2 ATP (net) + 2 NADH
■ anaerobic, doesn't need O2
○ Krebs cycle (aka citric acid cycle)
■ in the mitochondrial matrix
■ pyruvate → CO2 (this is the CO2 we breathe out!!)
■ makes 2 ATP, NADH, FADH2
○ Electron transport chain
■ inner membrane of mitochondria (cristae)
■ NADH + FADH2 drop off electrons → pumps H+ ions
■ O2 = final electron acceptor → makes water
■ ~32-34 ATP (most of it!)
● Total ≈ 36-38 ATP per glucose
● No oxygen → fermentation
○ lactic acid (muscles, yogurt) — why legs burn when you sprint
○ alcoholic (yeast, bread) — CO2 + ethanol
○ only 2 ATP, way less efficient
● ask Mr. P: why does the textbook say 30-32 ATP but the slides say 36-38??
● quiz Fri on the stages + where each one happens`,
