    text: `Kira Nakamura
Mr. Delacroix
Honors English 10
4 November 2026

Annotated Bibliography: Can Coral Reefs Be Restored?

Alvarez, Jonah. "Gardening the Sea: Inside Florida's Coral Nurseries." Ocean Today, 12 Mar. 2025, www.oceantoday.example.org/gardening-the-sea.
This magazine article describes how volunteers and scientists in the Florida Keys grow fragments of staghorn and elkhorn coral on underwater "trees" made of PVC pipe and then transplant them onto damaged reefs. Alvarez interviews several nursery managers and includes statistics on how many fragments have been planted since 2010. The article is written for a general audience, so it is easy to understand, but it is more optimistic than the scientific sources and does not discuss failure rates in much detail. I will use it for background and to explain how coral gardening works.

Bennett, Laura, et al. "Survival of Outplanted Corals After Marine Heatwaves." Journal of Reef Ecology, vol. 14, no. 3, 2024, pp. 211–29.
This peer-reviewed study tracked more than 8,000 transplanted corals at sites in the Caribbean over six years. The researchers found that most outplanted corals survived the first two years, but a single marine heatwave in 2023 killed over 70 percent of them at some sites. The authors argue that restoration cannot succeed unless ocean warming is slowed. This is the most important source for my counterargument because it shows the limits of restoration, and since it is peer-reviewed it is highly credible.

Coral Restoration Alliance. "Our Approach." Coral Restoration Alliance, 2026, www.coralrestorationalliance.example.org/approach.
This page from a nonprofit explains the group's methods, including breeding heat-tolerant corals and using microfragmentation to make corals grow faster. It is useful for describing new techniques, but because the organization raises money for its own projects, it may present its results in the best possible light. I will use it carefully and check its claims against the scientific articles.

Okafor, Daniel. Reefs in the Balance: Science, Hope, and the Future of Coral. Tidewater Press, 2022.
Okafor is a marine biologist who has studied reefs for thirty years, and this book gives a broad overview of coral biology, the threats reefs face, and the different strategies for saving them. Chapter 7, on restoration, was the most helpful because it compares coral gardening, assisted evolution and larval seeding and explains the cost of each. The book is a few years old, so I will look for newer data, but it gives a strong scientific foundation for my paper.

Reyes, Carmen. "Why Saving Reefs Means Saving Fishing Towns." The Coastal Review, 8 July 2025, www.coastalreview.example.com/reefs-fishing-towns.
This opinion piece argues that coral restoration projects should hire local fishers and divers so that communities benefit economically. Reyes gives examples from the Philippines and Belize. Because it is an opinion article it is not a neutral source, but it adds a human and economic side to my topic that the scientific sources leave out. I may use it in my conclusion.`,
