    name: "College APA annotated bibliography on later school start times",
    expect: "annotated",
    text: `Annotated Bibliography: Later School Start Times and Adolescent Health
Jordan Whitaker
PSY 240: Adolescent Development
Dr. Nkemelu
November 18, 2026

Cardenas, M. R., & Whitley, J. (2023). Delayed school start times and adolescent sleep duration: A three-year longitudinal study. Journal of Adolescent Health Research, 41(2), 155–170.
Cardenas and Whitley followed about 2,400 students in two neighboring suburban districts before and after one district moved its high school start time from 7:30 to 8:30 a.m. Students in the district that changed reported sleeping an average of 34 minutes longer on school nights, and the gain held steady over three years rather than fading as critics had predicted. The comparison district and the long follow-up make this one of the strongest studies on the question, although sleep duration was self-reported rather than measured. I will use this source as the core of my section on sleep outcomes.

Delgado, P. (2022). The bus problem: Transportation costs and the politics of school schedules. Education Policy Quarterly, 18(4), 402–419.
Delgado examines why many districts that consider later start times end up rejecting them, using case studies of six districts in three states. The main obstacle in every case was bus routing: districts that run elementary, middle and high schools on staggered schedules with the same buses must either buy more buses or flip the order so that younger children start earlier. Delgado also documents opposition from parents who rely on teenagers to watch younger siblings after school and from coaches worried about practice time. The article does not measure health outcomes, but it explains the practical trade-offs that my policy recommendation will need to address.

Hoang, T., Fairbanks, E., & Mwangi, S. (2024). Sleep, mood, and school start time: Evidence from a natural experiment. Developmental Psychology Today, 12(1), 33–51.
Using survey data from a statewide change in start-time policy, Hoang and colleagues found that later start times were associated with fewer reported symptoms of depression and lower daytime sleepiness, especially among students who had previously slept fewer than seven hours a night. The effects on mood were modest but statistically significant. Because the policy was imposed by the state rather than chosen by districts, the authors argue that their results are less likely to reflect differences between communities. This source supports my argument that start times affect mental health, not only sleep.

Ivers, R. (2021). Biological clocks and the teenage brain. In L. Santos (Ed.), Foundations of adolescent development (2nd ed., pp. 88–112). Meridian Academic Press.
This textbook chapter explains why adolescents tend to fall asleep and wake later than children and adults, including the delayed nighttime release of melatonin during puberty. It is a secondary source and presents no new research, but it gives clear background I can use in my introduction to explain why early start times conflict with teenagers' natural sleep patterns.

Park, J. Y. (2025). Do later start times improve grades? A meta-analysis. Review of Educational Outcomes, 9(3), 270–295.
Park combines the results of 27 studies on school start times and academic performance. Overall, later start times were linked to small improvements in grades and attendance, with larger gains in first-period classes, but results varied widely between studies. Park cautions that many of the included studies were short and did not control for other changes happening in schools at the same time. This meta-analysis gives a balanced picture and will keep me from overstating the academic benefits.`,
