    name: "Call of the Wild reading questions, answered in sentences",
    expect: "homework",
    text: `The Call of the Wild — Chapters 1–3 Reading Questions
Name: Gabriel Ortiz     Date: 10/8     Per. 6
Answer in complete sentences. Use evidence from the text.

1. Where does Buck live at the beginning of the novel, and what is his life like there?
Buck lives on Judge Miller's large estate in the Santa Clara Valley in California. His life is easy and comfortable. He rules over the other dogs, goes swimming and hunting with the Judge's sons, and is treated almost like royalty.

2. Why is Buck kidnapped? Who takes him?
Manuel, one of the gardener's helpers, steals Buck and sells him because he has gambling debts. Big strong dogs like Buck are suddenly worth a lot of money because men in the Klondike gold rush need them to pull sleds.

3. What lesson does Buck learn from the man in the red sweater?
The man in the red sweater beats Buck with a club until he stops attacking. Buck learns that a man with a club is a lawgiver who has to be obeyed, even if he does not have to be liked. This is the first rule of his new life.

4. Describe what happens to Curly. What does Buck learn from her death?
Curly is a friendly Newfoundland who tries to make friends with a husky. The husky attacks her, and when she falls the other dogs pile on and kill her. Buck learns that in this new world there is no fair play, and once you go down you are finished. He decides he will never go down.

5. How does Buck change in Chapter 2? Give two examples.
Buck becomes tougher and more practical. One example is that he learns to dig a hole in the snow to sleep in, like the other dogs. Another example is that he starts stealing food when no one is looking, which he never would have done at Judge Miller's. The narrator says his old morals were useless in the North.

6. Why do Buck and Spitz hate each other?

7. Explain the idea of "survival of the fittest" and how it connects to Chapter 3.`,
